# Feature pipeline: from idea to "ticket it and queue it"

This file's instructions are duplicated, pithily, into `coordinator-core.md` for a harness that cannot load this file directly.
TheForge's coordinator-core ADR records why that file exists and what must survive every edit to it.
A change here updates `.forge/coordinator-core.md` in the same change.
TheForge's `lock` check fails until the core section citing this one is re-read and the lock refreshed.

Every new feature goes through the same stages, in order, each leaving a record the next points at; skipping one is how a cheap implementation agent ends up guessing.
Stage 0 runs once per repo, before the first feature; after that a feature starts at stage 1.

This file is the reference; `.agents/skills/feature-pipeline/SKILL.md` enforces it.
`grill-me` (stage 2) adds a nine-item checklist to the feature issue, so the stages that are conversations rather than tickets are tracked somewhere cheap.
`to-tickets` (stage 6) files a post-merge review pass ticket alongside the build slices, plus a stage 8 architecture pass ticket blocked on it, except for a single-slice design, which gets only the post-merge review pass ticket.
A feature whose stage 3 thread invokes the single-ticket discernment (stage 3, below) skips stage 6's ticket-filing step, not the stages after it: the feature issue is already its build ticket, stage 7 proceeds from it directly, and the discernment fires `feature-pipeline`'s job (b) directly for the follow-up filing, with the single-slice-design result of a post-merge review pass ticket only.
A feature's own issue is the pipeline's record.

## Stage 0: project inception (once per repo)

Stage 0 is a conversation with the owner, not a silent script: before its first ask, the thread gives a short plain-language orientation: what stage 0 is for, that a handful of one-time setup offers come first and then an interview in rounds, and why the answers matter (they seed `docs/principles.md`, settle the guard tooling, and set the project's lint/test/coverage commands).
It keeps narrating through stage 0's sub-steps (the tracker/label scaffolding, each setup offer, the grilling interview, writing `{{ local_full_suite_script }}`), saying in a line what it does next and why, rather than previewing once and falling silent until done.

Before the first feature, scaffold the issue tracker, triage labels and domain-doc layout the other skills assume (the bootstrap script does most of this; `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md`, `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` and `docs/agents/domain.md` describe the result).
Offer to run the `impeccable` skill's setup to capture durable product context for the project's UI work: ask once whether to run it now or skip it, say so plainly if skipped, and offer it again the first time a feature introduces or changes a UI.
That product context captures product truth (users, purpose, positioning, constraints) and points to `CONTEXT.md` for domain vocabulary rather than duplicating its definitions.
Offer the `doc-build-setup` skill's setup (the project's document/diagram build pipeline) and the `process-map-setup` skill's setup (process map and skills-index generation) in the same ask-once, skip-and-say-so shape.
Offer stack-specific skills the same way, once per matching stack, from a small stack → skills table whose one row today is TypeScript → `setup-ts-deep-modules` and `migrate-to-shoehorn`.
On opt-in, write a "TypeScript conventions" rule into `CLAUDE.md` citing both skills by name, seed each skill's `docs/tools/process-map.yaml` entry without `discretionary: true` and with a why edge to that rule, regenerate `docs/process-map.md` and `docs/agents/skills-index.md` with `process_map.py build` and `process_map.py index`, and run `process_map.py check` to confirm.
When `process-map-setup` was skipped, write the `CLAUDE.md` rule now and note on the skip record that both skills' process-map entries are owed once it runs.
On decline, both skills stay `discretionary: true`.
A later stack adds its own row to the table, not new prose.

Then a grilling thread (`grill-me`, which runs `grilling`) draws out with the owner the principles every feature must respect.
Before the first round, it walks the owner through the principles `docs/principles.md` already carries at generation, saying plainly that the owner can change or add to any of them, at stage 0 or later, through `domain-modeling`'s owner-approval process.
The interview covers what in the domain will change underneath the project, which external systems are trusted and how far, which frameworks are built on rather than written, size and complexity budgets, test philosophy, and security posture.
Each question states in a line why it is asked, on top of `grilling`'s shared round format, which doesn't require a why.
Each principle whose exact wording the owner approves is written into `docs/principles.md` (the principles part of `domain-modeling`), with its why and enforcement, in one ticketed docs PR quoting the owner's approval of that wording.
Stage 0 also chooses the project's real tools for the generic guards (linter, dead-code tool, size-cap measurement, test-coverage tool), sets the `coverage_floor` answer from a measured baseline of the project's own suite, and replaces the template's placeholder commands.
It also writes `{{ local_full_suite_script }}`, the project's local stand-in for the gap between scheduled CI passes (`.forge/ci-and-checks.md`, which also says how stage 0 sets its answers when its name or switch differs from the defaults; `watch-main-ci`'s Routine 2 names its purpose, schedule and evidence format), rather than leaving it for Main Health to discover later.
`docs/principles.md` on `main` holding at least one owner-confirmed principle beyond the forge-seeded ones is the record that stage 0 has run.
Stage 0 has no line on a feature's checklist.

## Stage 1: idea

A `future` issue under the deferred-ideas epic {{ ideas_ref }} records the problem, the owner's own words, and the open questions, and carries no design.
A new idea is parked only when the owner says it is deferred; if the owner's words don't say so, or are unclear, ask whether it is for now or deferred before filing it, and never assume.
The moment the owner says to start the pipeline, the issue is detached from the deferred-ideas epic and loses the `future` label in that same action.

## Stage 2: grilling for use cases

A grilling thread (`grill-me`) with the owner elicits the high-level use cases and product constraints (scope, delivery, performance, who may do what) and records them on the issue: use cases only, no implementation.
As terms settle, the thread resolves them into the `CONTEXT.md` glossary right there, challenging a term that collides with an existing one and sharpening a fuzzy one into a canonical term, so a collision between near-identical terms is caught here, not at stage 3 or 4.
ADRs are stage 3's job, which is why `grill-with-docs` (grilling plus all of `domain-modeling`, ADRs included) is not the skill for stage 2.

## Stage 3: high-level design

> Before any other work, check the model actually serving this turn against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
> In Claude Code on the web, for example, call `get_session` with no id and read `session_context.model` and `external_metadata.last_served_model`; another harness has its own equivalent of "what model is actually running this turn."
> If it is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
> A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
> Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.

Before anything else, this thread removes `ready-for-feature-design` from the feature issue, with no replacement label, via the mechanical-tier label-sync sub-agent (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Mechanical label sync after design-type work"); the tracker carries no ready label again until stage 6 labels its slice tickets, except where the single-ticket discernment below makes the tracker itself the build ticket.

A design thread (design-tier, which never implements) posts one design comment on the issue, carrying:

- facts verified against `origin/main` with file references, the architecture, the decisions, and the proposed requirement wording (short, one "shall" each);
- a security section (new attack surface, trust boundaries crossed, data stored or exposed, and the STRIDE threats raised with their controls), written against `docs/security/threat-model.md` using `security-docs`' delta mode;
- a check against the `impeccable` skill when the design introduces or changes a user interface;
- the ADRs the design needs, drafted as text.

A non-security risk the design accepts or leaves open is filed as a `needs-decision` ticket and linked.
When the design's central approach is a problem with known outside solutions (a protocol, an algorithm, an established UX or architecture pattern) the repo doesn't settle, the design thread delegates a prior-art lookup to `research`, cites the findings in the design comment, and lands the findings file with the design doc at stage 5, next to its ADR as `docs/adr/NNNN-<slug>.research.md` or, when it informed no ADR, next to the design doc as `docs/design/<slug>.research-<topic>.md`; findings are never left only in a comment or on a throwaway branch.

The design thread asks the owner product questions one at a time or in batches, as suits the owner, each as a decision card following "Every approval request links to the wording it asks about" below.
Each owner decision gets its own amendment comment saying what it supersedes.
Tick checklist item 3 in the same edit that posts the design comment.

**Stage 3 discernment for a whole feature in one ticket, low drift risk.**
The stage 3 thread may itself judge, with no owner decision card, that the whole feature (not a slice of a larger one) is achievable in one ticket with low risk of architectural drift, and record that judgement to skip stages 5 and 8.
It is whole-feature-only: a feature needing more than one slice, or where only one of several slices would qualify as low-risk, does not qualify, and a single low-risk slice inside a multi-slice feature is routed through `to-tickets`'s per-slice criteria at stage 6 instead.

The test is conjunctive: none of the five `ready-for-structure` routing criteria in `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` apply to the feature's design, and the whole feature fits in one ticket end to end.
The design comment names which of the five criteria it checked and found not to apply, not merely "none apply".

The feature issue itself becomes the build ticket: no child ticket is filed through `to-tickets`, and stage 6 does not run for this feature.
In the same edit that posts the design comment, the thread ticks checklist item 5 as skipped (`[x] 5. Detailed design (skipped — stage 3 discernment)`) and hands `feature-pipeline`'s job (b) to a build-tier sub-agent directly (the "fire it directly ... outside those two paths" case its frontmatter names), treating the feature issue as the sole, terminal build slice.
Job (b) files the `Post-merge review pass for <feature>` ticket blocked by the feature issue, and its existing single-slice-design shortcut skips the architecture-pass ticket and ticks checklist item 9 as skipped, so no new logic is needed there.
Checklist item 8 (the post-merge review pass) is never skipped by this discernment: a whole feature built as one ticket gets the same post-merge review as a single-slice design doc, never less.
Job (b) also ticks checklist item 6 once it has filed, or deliberately skipped, every follow-up ticket it was going to file, as for a single-slice design; item 6 gets a plain tick, not a skip annotation like items 5 and 9, because this feature still gets exactly the ticket-filing work item 6 represents.
Only once items 5, 6 and 9 are ticked does that pass start the mechanical-tier label-sync sub-agent (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Mechanical label sync after design-type work") to apply `ready-for-build` to the feature issue directly; its `ready-for-feature-design` was already removed at the start of stage 3, so there is no ready label to flip, only one to gain.
The one issue then carries both the pipeline checklist and the ordinary ticket `Requirements:`/`Tests:`/`Review:` fields.
Stage 4 (requirements approval) still runs as normal before the build starts.

## Stage 4: requirements approval

The owner approves the requirement wording on the issue, through a request that links to the stage 3 comment (or the spec-only PR's diff, once it exists) and quotes or summarises the wording inline.
When the design changes no requirement's wording, stage 4 is a no-op: tick checklist item 4 in the same edit that posts the stage 3 comment, with a one-line note that no wording changed, and go straight to stage 5.
Otherwise the stage 3 design thread itself, once the owner's approval comment is recorded, opens the spec-only PR citing it and merges it (`.forge/requirements-driven-changes.md`).
It is not handed to a separate thread: transcribing already-approved wording is mechanical, not design judgement, and SDLC-011 treats a strict design/build split as desirable rather than required, so a thread doesn't sit idle waiting on a handoff for work this small.
The design thread may delegate the literal diff edit to a mechanical-tier sub-agent, but keeps the PR, the review gate and the merge, since it can confirm the merged diff matches the linked, approved wording verbatim.
The review gate runs in full whichever thread opens the PR (`.forge/review-gate.md`); this changes who merges it, never what clears it.
Status changes and ADRs need no approval; requirement wording does (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge").
Tick checklist item 4 in the same action as that PR's merge, or with the no-op note above.

## Stage 5: detailed design

> Before any other work, check the model actually serving this turn against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
> In Claude Code on the web, for example, call `get_session` with no id and read `session_context.model` and `external_metadata.last_served_model`; another harness has its own equivalent of "what model is actually running this turn."
> If it is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
> A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
> Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.

Stage 5 runs in the same design thread as stage 3 (or 4), with no new brief or re-queue between them, so this is normally a repeat of a check already cleared this session; a thread picking stage 5 up fresh (a retrofit, a rotation) cannot skip it on that assumption.

The design thread writes `docs/design/<slug>.md` and the ADR files in one docs PR (`Requirements: none (internal)`), written for the agents that will build it in the `codebase-design` vocabulary and the `writing-for-agents` rules.
When the feature introduces a new incident type, the doc names the runbook it needs under `docs/security/runbooks/`.

The design doc has these parts:

- the invariants every slice keeps;
- a module map: new modules, and the one named hook in each touched module;
- for a design with more than one slice, a shared-idioms table, written before the slices are cut: one row per cross-cutting concern two or more slices will meet (how a key is built, how a write is made, where a shared query is defined, how a "still current" check is made, and similarly), naming the one module and helper every slice uses for it and the slices that meet it;
- the data model and configuration keys;
- a build-versus-buy line for every new module: the library considered and why it was rejected, or the library chosen instead of hand-rolling (`.forge/code-shape-rules.md`, "Adopt the abstraction early, then stay on it");
- when that line depends on facts about an external library or API, the design thread delegates the reading to `research` and cites its findings in the line;
- such a decision gets an ADR, drafted in the stage 3 comment and landed with the design doc, with the findings kept beside it (next to its ADR as `docs/adr/NNNN-<slug>.research.md`, or, when it informed no ADR, next to the design doc as `docs/design/<slug>.research-<topic>.md`);
- one section per implementation slice, each with its purpose, module and interface (signatures, error modes), behaviour, the seam it is tested through, and the guard test that must go red when the check is removed;
- in each slice section, a threat-model delta: the proposed rows for `docs/security/threat-model.md` and any runbook needed, carrying forward stage 3's security section, or "Security: none" with a one-line reason;
- in each slice section, a UI check against the `impeccable` skill when the slice introduces or changes a user interface, or "UI: none" with a one-line reason;
- in each slice section, its file scope, blocking edges and requirement IDs;
- a test matrix;
- open items the implementer must re-verify.

File paths and signatures belong here, unlike a ticket body, because this document constrains the implementation.
Before the doc PR opens, the design thread applies `codebase-design`'s own tests to its module map: the deletion test on every new module, and "one adapter is a hypothetical seam, two adapters make a real one" on every new protocol or adapter, with a one-line justification for each seam it keeps.
For a multi-slice design, it also checks the shared-idioms table against the slice sections: every concern two or more slices meet has exactly one row, each row names one module and one helper, and no slice section describes its own way of solving a concern a row names.
A row's helper that does not exist yet appears in the module map and is built by one slice, which every other slice in that row blocks on.
The doc PR does not open while a cross-cutting concern is unsettled; leaving it for each slice to answer is what this check prevents (`docs/principles.md`, "Don't repeat yourself").
Every module-map and build-vs-buy row must be claimed by some slice; `to-tickets` checks this at stage 6, and the design thread can run the same cross-check early to self-check cheaply.
Tick checklist item 5 in the same action as the design doc PR's merge.
Stage 5 applies no label of its own to the tracker, which stays an `epic` until stage 6 labels its slice tickets (its `ready-for-feature-design` was removed at the start of stage 3).

The design doc PR needs no owner approval: a design doc and its ADRs merge once the review gate is clear (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "What needs the owner's approval before a merge").
Stage 4's requirement wording is no exception to the stage order: the design doc PR merges only after stage 4's spec-only PR has.
If the design needs another item on that closed list (a principle's wording, say, or lowering a coverage floor), only that item waits: put it to the owner with the exact wording linked and quoted ("Every approval request links to the wording it asks about" below), carry it in its own PR, and let the rest of the design doc merge.

## Stage 6: ticket it and queue it

When the owner says so, the coordinator arranges for `to-tickets` to run against the design doc.
The owner's word schedules the build; it is not a further approval of the design, which stages 3 to 5 settled, and `to-tickets` skips its own breakdown-approval step here, since one slice is one ticket.
Stage 6 always waits for that word, because it decides when the build takes queue slots; nothing replaces it or infers it from an earlier approval.

So the feature never sits unnoticed between stages, the design thread asks for the word itself in the same action as the design doc PR's merge, and stays open on that card: one decision card, "Ticket and queue <feature> now?", linking the merged design doc and listing its slices, with options to ticket it now or hold it.
It records the owner's answer on the feature issue in the approval-record form (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Evidence: where each record lives") and tells the coordinator, whichever way it went.
The coordinator's standing arrangement is that the design thread that wrote the doc runs the ticketing, since it holds the doc.
On "ticket it now" the design thread proceeds without waiting to be re-briefed, handing `to-tickets` to a build-tier sub-agent, because ticketing is build-tier work whatever thread orchestrates it.
When the owner gives the word elsewhere, such as the project chat, the coordinator relays it to that thread, which proceeds the same way.
On "hold it", the thread's job ends and it is resolved; when the owner later gives the word, the coordinator reopens it or briefs a fresh design thread with the doc to run the ticketing.
One slice is normally one ticket; a slice is split only along a seam the doc already names.
An orphan found by `to-tickets`' cross-check of the module map and build-vs-buy rows sends the doc back to stage 5 before any ticket is filed.
Each ticket points at its slice and adds only the standing brief rules (merge rule, scoped tests, requirement IDs, "Blocked by").
Each ticket is labelled `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure` per `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`'s routing criteria, and tickets are handed out one at a time per build thread.
A slice of this stage 5 design doc defaults to `ready-for-build` under that routing criteria's narrow carve-out, even where criteria 1-5 would otherwise match its subject matter.
A slice whose design-doc section left the question open goes back to stage 5 instead, never to a design-exit pass for nested design inside the ticket.
The owner's stage 6 word is the acceptance of every ticket this stage files.
The design thread reports the filed tickets to the coordinator in the same action that ticks item 6; the coordinator then labels each unblocked one `queued` and points its `Request-trace:` line at the feature issue, with no further owner word, and queues a blocked one the moment its blockers close (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "The label state machine").
No further owner word starts stage 7: the slot limits and the coordinator's hand-out order alone decide when each queued slice is built.

## Stage 7: build

Each ticket is built with `implement-with-merge`, re-invoked in full at the start of each ticket.
A thread that has built several tickets re-reads `implement-with-merge`, `implement` and `tdd`'s guard-removal section before the first test of every next one, because test discipline decays across a long thread.
When implementation has to deviate from the design doc, the PR updates the doc's section in the same PR and says why in its body; the doc is the record of what was built.
A slice meeting a cross-cutting concern the shared-idioms table does not name does not solve it locally: it goes back to stage 5 to add the row first, like any other question its section left open.
A gap the builder finds outside the slice's scope is not built and not queued: it is raised to the owner as a `needs-decision` ticket, or filed as a `deferred-arch-finding` when weak (SDLC-116).
A slice pauses for an owner approval only when it needs an item on the closed approval list, such as a requirement whose wording must change before the slice can satisfy it; that slice waits, with the exact wording linked and quoted, while the feature's other unblocked slices keep building.
A `needs-decision` label or an explicit owner hold still stops its own ticket, as anywhere else.
A design-doc update for a deviation, the terminal follow-up PR below and the post-merge review pass need no merge approval from the owner, so none waits for one.

A slice is terminal when no other slice in the feature's graph blocks on it, and a feature with parallel branches can have more than one.
When the merged slice is terminal and every other terminal slice is already merged, a small follow-up PR, through the ordinary branch-and-review path, makes these edits right after that merge, never inside the slice's own PR and never as a direct write to `main`:

- the design doc's status line says "implemented, reconciled against main @ <sha>", the architecture description and user guide carry the feature from then on, and `implement-with-merge` ticks checklist item 7;
- a short-lived design-tier sub-agent folds every slice's threat-model delta into `docs/security/threat-model.md` and any other security doc it names, using `security-docs`' delta mode.
  Before any other work, that sub-agent runs the tier gate:

  > Before any other work, check the model actually serving this turn against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
  > In Claude Code on the web, for example, call `get_session` with no id and read `session_context.model` and `external_metadata.last_served_model`; another harness has its own equivalent of "what model is actually running this turn."
  > If it is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
  > A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
  > Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.

This follow-up PR still needs a ticket (the feature issue backs it, so none is filed) and the review gate (`.forge/review-gate.md`), like any other change.
The light gate fits only the status-line and checklist-tick edit on its own; a follow-up also carrying a threat-model fold gets the full gate, because new threat and control rows are not a prose-only edit changing no rule, requirement or behaviour.
A thread never skips the PR path to save that review.

The post-merge review pass ticket was filed at stage 6, blocked by every terminal build slice, so finishing stage 7 unblocks it.
It runs before stage 8, so the architecture pass reviews code the post-merge review has already seen.
The standing post-merge review cadence (`.forge/post-merge-review.md`) is a safety net under this ticket, never a substitute.

## Stage 8: architecture pass

The architecture pass ticket was filed at stage 6, blocked by the post-merge review pass ticket, so it surfaces on the queue once that pass is done.
For a single-slice design this stage is skipped outright: no ticket exists, nobody runs the pass, and the feature issue's checklist records the skip.

Otherwise, before any other work, the fresh design-tier thread briefed for this pass runs the tier gate:

> Before any other work, check the model actually serving this turn against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
> In Claude Code on the web, for example, call `get_session` with no id and read `session_context.model` and `external_metadata.last_served_model`; another harness has its own equivalent of "what model is actually running this turn."
> If it is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
> A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
> Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.

That thread, briefed with the design doc and the feature issue's design comments, runs `improve-codebase-architecture` scoped to the files the design doc's slices named or touched, never the whole codebase, once per feature.
For any user interface in that scope it also checks for drift from stage 3 and 5's UI check against the `impeccable` skill.
For a multi-slice design, it also runs that skill's shared-idiom check against the design doc's shared-idioms table: a named idiom a slice bypassed without a reason stated at the site, or a concern two slices each solved with no row naming it, is graded Strong by default, the first departing from the approved design and the second being the duplicate the table exists to prevent.

The same thread triages every finding into exactly one of four outcomes, with no fifth:

- a Strong finding is fixed now;
- a rejected finding with a load-bearing reason becomes an ADR, so a later pass does not raise it again;
- a finding worth exploring is raised to the owner as an open question, a `needs-decision` ticket, and the owner decides whether it is fixed, recorded as an ADR or parked;
- a weak finding becomes a `deferred-arch-finding` issue under the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }} that names the finding and the files.

`deferred-arch-finding` carries both provenance (an automated review pass, not the owner) and status (not actioned right now), keeping pass-sourced ideas out of the deferred-ideas epic, which holds only ideas the owner raised.
The thread posts the triage on the feature's issue, then hands the coordinator the fixes to queue as tickets, one per finding, each labelled by the routing criteria and built like any other slice.
Every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket the triage files is added as a sub-issue of the feature's tracker issue in the same action that files it, so the tracker reflects what the pass found; a `deferred-arch-finding` ticket stays under the architecture-findings epic {{ arch_ref }} only, being deliberately parked.
If two or more findings share a shape, the thread also asks the owner "is there a principle here?".
This gate is routine, not optional: a per-PR review cannot see what only the whole feature shows, such as a seam two slices each patched around, a helper added twice, or a hook that drifted from the doc.
The architecture-pass ticket closes once the triage is posted and every finding is filed, without waiting on any filed ticket's fix to merge.
The feature's checklist item for this stage is ticked only once the architecture-pass ticket and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are resolved, never when the triage is merely posted or the pass ticket alone closes.
A ticket is resolved when closed, or when it is a `needs-decision` ticket the owner has settled by parking it (for example as `hanging`, `deferred-accepted-risk` or `deferred-arch-finding`), with the owner's decision recorded on it and the ticket detached from the tracker.

## Closing the tracker

A feature tracker reaching 100% of its sub-issues closed is not by itself the signal to close it; before closing, also confirm three things.
Checklist items 8 and 9 are both ticked, meaning the passes actually ran rather than their tickets merely closing.
Every Must finding the post-merge review filed, and every Strong finding the architecture pass fixed now, has its fix PR merged, not merely filed.
Any owner-facing question either pass raised (a themed-bundle decision card, an "is there a principle here?" question) has been answered.
An open themed Should bundle or open `deferred-arch-finding` ticket does not block closing, but the closing comment says so and names those tickets so they keep their provenance.

## Every approval request links to the wording it asks about (Default)

Any request for the owner's decision on, or approval of, exact wording carries both of these in the request itself, not just somewhere earlier in the thread:

1. a direct link to the comment, PR diff or doc section holding the exact text being approved;
2. the wording itself, quoted verbatim, or faithfully summarised when the full text will not fit.

"Please approve the wording above" is not a request on its own; the owner should never have to hunt for the wording before acting on it.
ADRs and status changes need no approval, so this rule never applies to them.

## Which capability tier runs which stage

Cost follows capability: the tier that can get the judgement right once does the thinking, and a cheaper tier does the volume.
`.forge/orchestration.md` defines the tiers and `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps each tier to a model.

| Stage | Tier | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 0 Project inception | build-tier | An interview: the owner makes the calls and the thread records them as principles. |
| 1 Idea | any | Capturing the owner's words needs no judgement. |
| 2 Grilling for use cases | build-tier | An interview; glossary edits need no design judgement. |
| 3 High-level design, 5 Detailed design | design-tier | The decisions, the security reasoning and the module contracts are where a wrong call is expensive; this tier never implements. |
| 4 Requirements approval PR | build-tier (performed by the stage 3 thread itself, or skipped as a no-op) | Mechanical: it cites the owner's approval comment; the tier belongs to the work, not the thread that happens to run it. |
| 6 Ticket it and queue it | build-tier (a sub-agent of the stage 5 design thread by default) | `to-tickets` against a doc that already carries the design; the tier belongs to the work, not the thread that orchestrates it. |
| 7 Build and close | build-tier | One slice at a time, scoped tests, self-review, merge. |
| 7 Threat-model fold-in at the terminal merge | design-tier | `security-docs` never runs below design-tier. |
| 8 Architecture pass and its triage | design-tier, a fresh thread briefed with the design doc | Judging whether a seam is shallow across several merged slices is design judgement; the fixes it queues are built at build-tier. |
| Post-merge review pass | review-tier reviewers, orchestrated by a build-tier thread | A real review of every PR merged since the watermark. |
| Main Health | build-tier | Watching and reporting; design-tier only if a failure needs a root cause the slice owner cannot find. |
| Read-only sweeps, audits, listings, status checks, ticket housekeeping | mechanical-tier sub-agent | One obviously correct answer per check, and nothing is changed. |
